<p>Responding to PM request (even though I'm listed as not taking requests for the time being, but hey, 'tis the season, right?)</p>
<hr />
<p>So, I'm admittedly not super impressed here. I think there's a decent base to work with in regards to what the anomaly is capable of, which is shape-shifting into this very specific thing of art style mimicry. Giving it a personality centered around fascination of how humans express themselves through art is a good trait and can be tied into a narrative very neatly, I think.</p>
<p>There are, however, some snags that are keeping me from really enjoying this. There's this underlying murderous intent that doesn't make any sense to me. There's this drive to &quot;be good at art&quot; that feels contrived. And these are two big aspects of the character, making them kind of glaringly not appealing.</p>
<p>Which is what I want to focus on a bit. I think the character of the anomaly here needs a bit of shaping up and applied to a narrative that makes sense for it. If the anomaly's fascinations revolve around the history of human art, then what do its goals become? Are its efforts of artistic expression rooted in a desire to be human? Does it have a unique inner conflict that it can only express through mastery of an artistic medium that it has yet to find? People are often defined by their motivations and desires, which are often shaped by their past. What is this character trying to do, how does it get there, and what (if anything) gets in its way?</p>
<p>Feel free to reach out to me on <a href="/chat-guide">IRC</a> and we can talk this out a little more. I'm usually in all the chats, but it'd be better to hit me up in #thecritters.</p>

